    subtitle: "On the most damaging character in The Great Gatsby",
    body: `There is a habit among readers of The Great Gatsby, taught to them by generations of
teachers who learned it from earlier generations of teachers, of treating Nick Carraway
as the novel’s moral center. He is the one who sees clearly, the reader is told. He is the
one outside the corruption, observing it. He is the one who, by the final page, has earned
the right to call Gatsby “great” because he has watched the others and judged them and
found them wanting. This reading has the advantage of giving the novel a stable
narrator one can trust, and the further advantage of giving the reader a place to stand
inside the book that is not implicated in the wreckage of its plot. It also gets the novel
almost exactly wrong. Nick is not the moral center of The Great Gatsby. He is the most
damaging character in the book, and the damage he does is precisely the damage of
being treated as a moral center while doing the work of legitimizing everything he claims
to deplore.
Consider what Nick actually does, in plot terms, across the novel. He arranges, at
Gatsby’s request, the meeting between Gatsby and Daisy that initiates the affair. He sits
at the table at the Plaza Hotel during the confrontation that exposes the affair, and
offers neither intervention nor objection. He drives back to Long Island after Myrtle’s
death in a car driven by people he has accurately identified as careless, and he says
nothing. He attends Gatsby’s funeral and performs the ceremony of mourning without
ever connecting Gatsby’s death to the social arrangements—Daisy’s safety, Tom’s
impunity, the wealth that buys both—that produced it. At each major decision point in
the book, Nick has the option to do something other than observe, and at each one he
chooses observation. The novel’s tragedy is not that no one tried to stop it. It is that the
person best positioned to try, the narrator with full information and stated moral clarity,
declined to.
What is most damaging about Nick’s position, though, is not what he does but what
he provides. He provides the reader with a tone. The tone is dry, reflective, slightly
elevated—a tone that signals to the reader that the events being narrated are being

narrated by someone who understands them. The reader, encountering this tone, settles
into the comfortable position of being escorted through the wreckage by a competent
guide. This settling-in is the trap. Because Nick has provided the tone of moral clarity,
the reader receives the events as if they have already been judged. There is no need for
the reader to be disturbed; the narration is doing the disturbance for her, at a
manageable distance, in elegant prose. The result is that the reader leaves the novel
feeling that she has witnessed something serious, and feeling, by virtue of having
witnessed it through Nick’s eyes, somewhat implicated in his clarity rather than in the
actual events. Nick’s narration converts moral collapse into a literary experience. This is,
on examination, almost the opposite of what a moral center would do.
Look at the famous final paragraph. Nick stands on the lawn at night, contemplating
the green light, and produces some of the most quoted sentences in American literature.
The reader closes the book moved. The sentences are beautiful; the imagery is precise;
the cadence carries an unmistakable weight. But notice what the sentences accomplish
in argumentative terms. They convert the specific failure of Gatsby’s pursuit—and
behind it, the specific deaths of Gatsby and Myrtle, and the specific impunity of the
Buchanans—into a general meditation on American striving. The particulars dissolve
into the universal. Tom and Daisy, who have just driven away to their indestructible
wealth, become, in Nick’s framing, instances of the human condition rather than agents
of harm. The closing prose is so beautiful that it is rarely noticed how thoroughly it lets
the people responsible off the hook. The reader is left contemplating tides and green
lights and the past instead of contemplating what just happened to whom, and by whose
decisions.
There is a passage early in the novel that, on rereading, reveals what Nick is and
what the rest of the book will be. Nick describes Tom’s racism—his enthusiasm for a
book about the supposed decline of white civilization—and characterizes it as a
peculiarity, almost a quirk. There is irony in Nick’s description; the reader is meant to
understand that Tom’s racism is ridiculous. But notice how Nick’s irony functions. It
positions Nick above the racism, which the reader, identifying with Nick, also gets to be
above. The racism is named and dismissed in the same gesture. What is not done is any
examination of how Tom’s comfort in his racism is connected to his comfort in

everything else—his certainty of social position, his willingness to break things and let
other people fix them, his eventual freedom to walk away from Myrtle’s death. Nick
treats Tom’s worst quality as a curiosity rather than as the through-line it is. This is the
move he will perform repeatedly: noticing a thing, naming it ironically, declining to
follow its implications.
What is most insidious about Nick’s method is that it gives the reader a comfortable
place to inhabit while reading the novel. The reader, alongside Nick, gets to feel morally
engaged without being morally disturbed. She gets to disapprove of the Buchanans
without ever having to ask what would have to change for the Buchanans to be
impossible. The novel’s critique is preformatted; Nick has already done the disapproving
on the reader’s behalf, in a tone that makes the disapproval feel sophisticated. This
preformatting is what allows the novel to be assigned in tenth grade and consumed
without producing serious discomfort. The novel does not, in this reading, indict the
reader. It indicts Nick’s subjects, and the reader gets to stand with Nick. It is a brilliant
arrangement, and it is what has made the novel so durable in the curriculum. A novel
that disturbed the reader would not be assigned to ninth-graders. A novel whose
disturbance can be experienced from a safe vantage point can be.
What Fitzgerald appears to be doing, then—and the evidence accumulates across
rereadings—is using Nick to demonstrate the very thing Nick refuses to acknowledge:
that the most damaging form of complicity is the one performed by people who consider
themselves observers. Nick believes he is reporting on a world he is not part of. The
novel demonstrates that the reporting is itself the participation. Each act of beautiful
narration provides cover for the events being narrated; each ironic remark stabilizes the
very arrangements it appears to critique. By the time Nick stands on the lawn at the end,
what he has done is not witness the tragedy. He has aestheticized it. The reader who
treats him as the moral center has accepted the aestheticization as moral clarity. This is
the book’s actual subject, and it is one of the more unsettling subjects a novel has ever
taken on, which is why the novel has had to be domesticated, in classroom after
classroom, into the simpler story about the green light. The simpler story is what Nick
wants the reader to read. The harder story is what Fitzgerald wrote.`,
