    slug: "the-word-too-does-all-the-work",
    title: "The Word \u201cToo\u201d Does All the Work",
    subtitle: "On Robert Hayden\u2019s \u201cThose Winter Sundays\u201d and the cost of slowing down",
    body: `Robert Hayden’s poem “Those Winter Sundays” is fourteen lines long, and one of those
lines contains a word so small that most readers move past it without registering its
presence. The poem is widely anthologized; it is taught in high schools across the
country; it is often read in approximately ninety seconds. The ninety-second reading
produces a sufficient summary of the poem—a son remembering, with belated gratitude,
the father who got up early on cold mornings to warm the house and polish the son’s
shoes—and the summary is not wrong. But the poem is doing something the summary
cannot capture, and the something is concentrated in a single word, in a single line, in
the second stanza. The word is “too,” and almost everything the poem actually means is
in it. Reading a poem closely means slowing down long enough to notice when a small
word is doing the work of a large argument. “Those Winter Sundays” rewards this
slowing-down more than almost any short poem in the American canon.
The first stanza establishes the setting. The father rises in the cold dark, dresses, and
goes to work in the cold of the house. The poem reports this with the matter-of-fact
economy of a son recounting a routine he watched without participating in. The opening
lines are factual, almost reportorial: a man getting up, a man making fire. There is no
editorializing yet. The son, who is now the speaker, is still allowing the memory to
present itself before the adult comprehension begins to operate on it. The father’s hands
are described as cracked—a small specific detail that the speaker, as a child, must have
seen many times. The first stanza ends with one of the most quietly devastating lines in
twentieth-century American poetry, which is the assertion that no one ever thanked
him.
Then the second stanza begins, and within its first few words the poem turns. The
speaker is recounting his own behavior as a child: he would wake and dress slowly and
reluctantly. And then the line continues with the word that does everything. The speaker
reports that he feared the chronic angers of the house. The word that has not yet
appeared—the word that will reframe the entire poem when it does—is “too.” The line

says he feared the angers, too. He feared them in addition to something else. The
grammar of “too” requires another fearer, an antecedent, someone else who was also
afraid. The someone else is the father. The line, in three letters, has revealed what the
rest of the poem will be working out: the father, getting up in the cold dark, making fire,
polishing shoes, was also afraid of the chronic angers of the house. The acts of care were
performed by a frightened man.
What the word “too” does, then, is install in the poem a second emotional life the
first stanza did not contain. The father is no longer just dutiful; he is afraid. The fear is
not specified—the angers are not described in detail, the chronic-ness is not
explained—and the lack of specification is part of the poem’s achievement. The reader
knows enough to understand that something difficult was happening in this household,
that the father’s acts of care were performed inside a climate of fear shared by adult and
child, and that the child’s ability to receive the care was compromised by the climate.
This is an enormous amount of information to convey in three letters, and Hayden
conveys it without ever telling the reader what the angers were. The reader is given the
temperature of the house, not the source of the heat. The withholding is exactly right: a
poem that named the angers would have specified a particular family rather than
illuminating a structure many readers would recognize from their own.
The third stanza moves into the speaker’s adult reflection. He recalls speaking
indifferently to the father who had warmed the rooms and polished the good shoes as
well. The phrase “as well” functions, in this stanza, the way “too” functioned in the
second: it expands the inventory of unappreciated labor. The father was not just
warming the house. He was also doing this other thing, this further service, this
additional care that the child accepted without acknowledgment. The poem keeps
adding items to a ledger the child did not know he was being given. By the time the
reader reaches the closing question, the ledger is long and the child’s indifference has
been documented in precise terms. The poem is preparing the question that ends it, and
the preparation is what makes the question land.
The closing question—what did the speaker know, what did he know, about love’s
austere and lonely offices—is so often quoted that its precision is sometimes overlooked.

Notice the word “offices.” It is the right word, the necessary word, and almost any
synonym would have damaged the line. “Offices” means duties or services, especially
formal or ritual ones. It carries a faint ecclesiastical resonance—the offices of the church
are the prayers said at the hours of the day. The father’s acts of care were offices in this
older sense: small, repeated, formal, performed at fixed times, sustained without
expectation of acknowledgment. The word makes love sound like work. It also makes
love sound like ritual, which is itself a kind of love—the kind that does not announce
itself but appears every morning, in the dark, before the rest of the house has woken.
The child, the speaker is saying, did not know that this was what love looked like. He
thought love would arrive labeled. It arrived disguised as the warming of cold rooms.
What the poem demonstrates, finally, is a particular truth about belated
understanding. The speaker has acquired an understanding he could not have acquired
as a child. The understanding was not available then—not because the evidence was
missing but because the child lacked the framework that would have made the evidence
legible. The father’s fear, the father’s offices, the chronic angers, the polished shoes—all
of it was present, but the child was not equipped to read it. He reads it now, as an adult,
and the reading is the poem. This is what poems can do that other forms struggle to do
as economically: they can model the moment at which earlier experience becomes newly
legible, and they can do it in fourteen lines, on the strength of a three-letter word placed
in exactly the right position to detonate everything around it. The student who wants to
learn what close reading is for can do worse than to spend twenty minutes on the word
“too,” in Hayden’s poem, and notice that twenty minutes on the right word is more
analytical work than a ninety-second reading of the whole poem could ever be.`,
